) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\n**Problems**\n\n1. **`lower(u.email)` in the WHERE clause isn't sargable.** Wrapping the column in a function stops a plain index on `users.email` from being used. The database has to scan every row in `users` and apply `lower()` to each one. This is the biggest issue.\n   - Fix A: create a functional index, `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`. The query then stays as it is.\n   - Fix B: store emails already normalized, or use a case-insensitive type or collation (`citext` in Postgres, or a `_ci` collation in MySQL). Then compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'` against a normal (ideally unique) index.\n\n2. **`SELECT *` over a join.** It pulls every column from both tables, including any wide or sensitive ones. It also blocks index-only scans. And if both tables have columns with the same name (`id`, `created_at`), the duplicates cause ambiguity or silent overwrites in client code. List only the columns you need.\n\n3. **No index on `orders.user_id` (to verify).** The query looks up one user and then fetches that user's orders. That needs an index on `orders(user_id)`. Many databases don't create one automatically for a foreign key. Postgres doesn't, though MySQL/InnoDB does. Without it, the database scans the whole `orders` table.\n\n4. **No `LIMIT` or `ORDER BY`.** A user with many orders returns an unbounded result set. If this feeds a UI or API, add pagination, for example `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT 50`. A composite index on `orders(user_id, created_at)` would serve that well.\n\n**Suggested rewrite**\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.total, o.created_at, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org'   -- or plain `u.email = ...` with citext/normalized data\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n```\n\nThe column names in the rewrite are placeholders, since I only have this one file. I haven't seen the schema, the existing indexes, or an `EXPLAIN` plan. Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on the real tables to confirm items 1 and 3. If you share the DDL, I can tighten the recommendations.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}